    "fish": {
      "word": "FISH",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A counter, used in various games.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A name loosely applied in popular usage to many animals of diverse characteristics, living in the water.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An oviparous, vertebrate animal usually having fins and a covering scales or plates. It breathes by means of gills, and lives almost entirely in the water. See Pisces. Dipnoi, and Elasmobranchii or Selachians (sharks and skates). Formerly the leptocardia and Marsipobranciata were also included, but these are now generally regarded as two distinct classes, below the fishes.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "pl.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The twelfth sign of the zodiac; Pisces.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The flesh of fish, used as food.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Naut.) (a) A purchase used to fish the anchor. (b) A piece of timber, somewhat in the form of a fish, used to strengthen a mast or yard. fish line, fish pole, fish spear, fish-bellied. Age of Fishes. See under Age, n., 8. mashed potato, and made into the form of a small, round cake. [U.S.] one) swells out like the belly of a fish. Francis. on the Atlantic coast of the United States. It feeds largely on fish. pisciculture. hauling up the anchor to the gunwale of a ship. them easily. upon two meeting beams, plates, etc., at their junction; -- used largely in connecting the rails of railroads. order to ascend falls in a river. etc., used in angling. parasitic Copepoda, belonging to Caligus, Argulus, and other related genera. See Branchiura. or sound. etc. animals, as whales, seals, sharks, from cods' livers, etc. Scotopelia and Ketupa, esp. a large East Indian species (K. Ceylonensis). catching crabs, lobsters, etc. fish; a weir. [Local, U.S.] Bartlett. trowel. ripple, to catch fish descending the current. Knight. are dried and used as food, or in the arts, as for the preparation of isinglass. incredible narration. [Colloq. U.S.] Bartlett. fish from a boiler. (b) A perforated earthenware slab at the bottom of a dish, to drain the water from a boiled fish.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To attempt to catch fish; to be employed in taking fish, by any means, as by angling or drawing a net.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To seek to obtain by artifice, or indirectly to seek to draw forth; as, to fish for compliments. Any other fishing question. Sir W. Scott.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To catch; to draw out or up; as, to fish up an anchor.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To search by raking or sweeping. Swift.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To try with a fishing rod; to catch fish in; as, to fish a stream. Thackeray.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To strengthen (a beam, mast, etc.), or unite end to end (two timbers, railroad rails, etc.) by bolting a plank, timber, or plate to the beam, mast, or timbers, lengthwise on one or both sides. See Fish joint, under Fish, n. To fish the anchor. (Naut.) See under Anchor.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
